<!-- SECTION 4 — BB84 ANALYSIS -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s4" class="section">
<div class="section-num">Section 04</div>
<h2>BB84 Analysis — Key Rate and Detection Probability</h2>

<p>Let's quantify how BB84 works in practice.</p>

<div class="def">
  <div class="def-title">Key Generation Rate</div>
  Alice generates N&#8320; bits. After basis comparison, they share roughly N&#8321; &asymp; N&#8320;/2 bits (50% basis match). They publish N&#7465; &asymp; &alpha;N&#8321; bits for the security check, with &alpha; &isin; [0,1]. The secret key has size:
  <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
    <div class="eq">N &asymp; (1&minus;&alpha;)/2 &middot; N&#8320;</div>
    <div class="eq-note">&nbsp;&nbsp;Half the bits are lost to basis mismatch, a fraction &alpha; goes to the security check</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="def">
  <div class="def-title">Detection Probability</div>
  Given that Eve is eavesdropping, and Nₚ bits are published for checking:
  <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
    <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">P(detect)</div><div class="mstep-body">= P(at least one published bit has an error) = 1 − P(no errors)</div></div>
    <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">Each bit:</div><div class="mstep-body">P(no error on one bit | Eve eavesdropping) = 3/4</div></div>
    <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">Together:</div><div class="mstep-body">P(detect Eve) = 1 − (3/4)<sup>Nₚ</sup> &nbsp;→&nbsp; 1 as Nₚ → ∞</div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="note-box">
  <strong>Critical limitation:</strong> This analysis assumes a <em>perfect</em> channel. In reality, channels have noise (photon loss, detector errors, etc.) that causes errors even without an eavesdropper. The simple BB84 above does NOT work in the presence of such errors — post-processing is needed (see Section 5).
</div>

<!-- Interactive key rate calculator -->
<canvas id="cvKeyRate" width="560" height="200"></canvas>
<div class="canvas-caption">Secret key size vs. number of bits Alice generates, for different values of α.</div>
<div class="controls" style="gap:0.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;">
  <label style="font-size:0.8rem;color:var(--muted);">α = <input type="range" id="slAlpha" min="0" max="99" value="20" oninput="updateKeyRate()"></label>
  <span class="ctrl-val" id="vAlpha">0.20</span>
  <span style="font-size:0.78rem;color:var(--muted);">fraction published for security check</span>
</div>

<!-- Exercise 4.1 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 4.1</span><span class="ex-title">Secret key size</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>Alice generates N₀ = 10,000 bits. She uses α = 0.5 (half the kept bits for security check). How many bits are in the final secret key?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <span class="ans-label">Key size &asymp;</span>
      <input class="answer-input" id="a41" placeholder="bits" style="width:100px">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkApprox('a41', 2500, 'fb41')">Check</button>
      <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="showHint('h41')">show hint</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h41">N = (1 − α)/2 × N₀ = (1 − 0.5)/2 × 10000 = 0.5/2 × 10000 = 0.25 × 10000 = 2500 bits.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb41"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Exercise 4.2 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 4.2</span><span class="ex-title">Eve detection with 30 bits</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>If Nₚ = 30 bits are used for the security check, what is the probability that Eve is detected? Use P(detect) = 1 − (3/4)<sup>30</sup>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <span class="ans-label">P(detect) &asymp;</span>
      <input class="answer-input" id="a42" placeholder="e.g. 0.99" style="width:100px">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkApprox('a42', 1 - Math.pow(0.75,30), 'fb42')">Check</button>
      <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="showHint('h42')">show hint</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h42">(3/4)^30 &asymp; 0.00018. So P(detect) = 1 &minus; 0.00018 &asymp; 0.9998 &asymp; 99.98%.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb42"></div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<!-- SECTION 5 — POST-PROCESSING -->
<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s5" class="section">
<div class="section-num">Section 05</div>
<h2>BB84 + Post-Processing</h2>

<p>In the real world, quantum channels are noisy. Even without an eavesdropper, Bob may receive corrupted qubits. For example, a measurement device might flip a bit with probability p. This means that even with matching bases, some bits will be wrong.</p>

<div class="note-box">
  <strong>Real scenario:</strong> Alice has x<sub>A</sub> = [0,1,1,0,...] after basis matching. Bob has x<sub>B</sub> = x<sub>A</sub> ⊕ n where each noise bit nᵢ = 1 (error) with probability p and nᵢ = 0 (correct) with probability 1−p.
</div>

<h3>The Three Post-Processing Steps</h3>

<h4 style="color:var(--orange);font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.82rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:1.2rem 0 0.5rem;">Step 1 — Adaptive Security Check</h4>
<p>Instead of a hard threshold, estimate the channel error rate:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="eq">p̂ = N<sub>err</sub> / N<sub>p</sub></div>
  <div class="eq-note">&nbsp;&nbsp;where N<sub>err</sub> is the number of erroneous bits among the N<sub>p</sub> published bits</div>
  <div class="mstep" style="margin-top:0.5rem;"><div class="mstep-label">If p̂ > γ</div><div class="mstep-body">→ eavesdropper detected (threshold γ is set in advance). Abort.</div></div>
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">If p&#770; &le; &gamma;</div><div class="mstep-body">&rarr; proceed to error correction. The noise is considered acceptable.</div></div>
</div>

<h4 style="color:var(--orange);font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.82rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:1.2rem 0 0.5rem;">Step 2 — Error Correction</h4>
<p>Alice and Bob need to reconcile their strings. They use a classical error-correcting code, but it must be done carefully to avoid revealing x<sub>A</sub> to Eve.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">Alice encodes</div><div class="mstep-body">Using a systematic linear code: c<sub>A</sub> = [x<sub>A</sub> | p<sub>A</sub>] where p<sub>A</sub> is the parity part.</div></div>
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">Alice sends</div><div class="mstep-body">Only p<sub>A</sub> (the parity bits) over the public classical channel. This is secure because many x<sub>A</sub> values produce the same parity.</div></div>
  <div class="mstep"><div class="mstep-label">Bob constructs</div><div class="mstep-body">c<sub>B</sub> = [x<sub>B</sub> | p<sub>A</sub>] and decodes it to recover x<sub>A</sub> (if there are not too many errors).</div></div>
</div>

<div class="insight">
  <strong>Why is sending p<sub>A</sub> secure?</strong> The parity bits do not uniquely determine x<sub>A</sub>. There are many valid codewords with the same parity — Eve cannot recover x<sub>A</sub> from p<sub>A</sub> alone.
</div>

<h4 style="color:var(--orange);font-family:'IBM Plex Mono',monospace;font-size:0.82rem;letter-spacing:0.08em;text-transform:uppercase;margin:1.2rem 0 0.5rem;">Step 3 — Privacy Amplification</h4>
<p>Even if error correction succeeds, Eve may have gained some partial information about x<sub>A</sub> by eavesdropping (information leakage). Privacy amplification removes this partial knowledge.</p>

<div class="def">
  <div class="def-title">Privacy Amplification</div>
  Alice and Bob apply a shared <strong>hash function</strong> to their (now identical) string x<sub>A</sub>:
  <div class="math-block" style="margin-top:0.4rem;">
    <div class="eq">x = hash(x<sub>A</sub>)</div>
  </div>
  The output x is the final secret key. The hash compresses x<sub>A</sub> into a shorter string. The key insight: even if Eve knows a few bits of x<sub>A</sub>, she knows essentially nothing about hash(x<sub>A</sub>) if the hash is well-chosen. The information leakage can be estimated from p̂.
</div>

<div class="math-block">
  <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:0.8rem;font-size:0.82rem;">
    <div style="background:var(--blue-hl);border-radius:6px;padding:0.6rem;border-left:3px solid var(--accent);">
      <strong style="color:var(--accent);">Security Check</strong><br>
      Estimate p̂ from Nₚ published bits.<br>If p̂ > γ → abort.
    </div>
    <div style="background:var(--green-hl);border-radius:6px;padding:0.6rem;border-left:3px solid var(--green);">
      <strong style="color:var(--green);">Error Correction</strong><br>
      Exchange parity bits publicly.<br>Bob recovers x<sub>A</sub>.
    </div>
    <div style="background:var(--purple-hl);border-radius:6px;padding:0.6rem;border-left:3px solid var(--purple);">
      <strong style="color:var(--purple);">Privacy Amplification</strong><br>
      Apply hash function.<br>x = hash(x<sub>A</sub>) → secret key.
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Exercise 5.1 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 5.1</span><span class="ex-title">Purpose of privacy amplification</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>After error correction, Alice and Bob share the same string x<sub>A</sub>. But Eve may know some bits of x<sub>A</sub>. Why does applying hash(x<sub>A</sub>) help?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <input class="answer-input wide" id="a51" placeholder="explain...">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkContains('a51',['hash','partial','information','knowledge','leakage','shorter'],'fb51')">Check</button>
      <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="showHint('h51')">show hint</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h51">A good hash is a many-to-one function: many inputs map to the same output. Even if Eve knows some bits of x_A, she cannot determine hash(x_A). The hash "amplifies" Eve's partial ignorance into complete ignorance of the final key.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb51"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Exercise 5.2 -->
<div class="ex-block">
  <div class="ex-header"><span class="ex-tag">Ex 5.2</span><span class="ex-title">Why is error correction needed?</span></div>
  <div class="ex-body">
    <p>If Alice and Bob share raw strings that differ in a few bits, can they just skip error correction and use privacy amplification directly?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="answer-area">
    <div class="answer-row">
      <input class="answer-input wide" id="a52" placeholder="yes / no, because...">
      <button class="btn sm" onclick="chkContains('a52',['no','different','same','identical','mismatch'],'fb52')">Check</button>
      <button class="hint-toggle" onclick="showHint('h52')">show hint</button>
    </div>
    <div class="feedback hint" id="h52">No. Privacy amplification requires Alice and Bob to apply the SAME hash to the SAME input. If their strings differ, they will compute different hashes and end up with different keys — the whole point of the protocol fails.</div>
    <div class="feedback" id="fb52"></div>
  </div>
</div>
</section>

<script is:inline>
/* ── Helpers ── */
function goTo(id){const el=document.getElementById(id);if(el)el.scrollIntoView({behavior:'smooth',block:'start'});}
function showHint(id){document.getElementById(id).classList.toggle('show');}
function setFb(id,ok,msg){const el=document.getElementById(id);if(!el)return;el.className='feedback show '+(ok?'ok':'no');el.textContent=msg;}
function norm(s){return s.trim().toLowerCase().replace(/\s/g,'').replace('−','-');}
function chk(iid,exp,fb){const v=norm(document.getElementById(iid).value);const ok=v===norm(exp)||(!isNaN(v)&&!isNaN(exp)&&Math.abs(parseFloat(v)-parseFloat(exp))<0.02);document.getElementById(iid).className='answer-input '+(ok?'ok':'no');setFb(fb,ok,ok?'✓ Correct!':'✗ Not quite — try again or show hint.');}
function chk2(i1,e1,i2,e2,fb){const ok1=norm(document.getElementById(i1).value)===norm(e1)||(!isNaN(document.getElementById(i1).value)&&Math.abs(parseFloat(document.getElementById(i1).value)-parseFloat(e1))<0.02);const ok2=norm(document.getElementById(i2).value)===norm(e2)||(!isNaN(document.getElementById(i2).value)&&Math.abs(parseFloat(document.getElementById(i2).value)-parseFloat(e2))<0.02);document.getElementById(i1).className='answer-input '+(ok1?'ok':'no');document.getElementById(i2).className='answer-input '+(ok2?'ok':'no');setFb(fb,ok1&&ok2,ok1&&ok2?'✓ Both correct!':'✗ Not quite.');}
function chkContains(iid,kws,fb){const v=document.getElementById(iid).value.toLowerCase().replace(/\s/g,'');const ok=kws.some(k=>v.includes(k));document.getElementById(iid).className='answer-input '+(ok?'ok':'no');setFb(fb,ok,ok?'✓ Correct!':'✗ Not quite — check the hint.');}
function chkApprox(iid,target,fb){const v=parseFloat(document.getElementById(iid).value.replace('%','').replace(',','.'));const t=typeof target==='number'?target:eval(target);const ok=!isNaN(v)&&Math.abs(v-(t>1?t:t*100>10?t:t))<0.06;const ok2=!isNaN(v)&&Math.abs(v-t*100)<2;document.getElementById(iid).className='answer-input '+((ok||ok2)?'ok':'no');setFb(fb,(ok||ok2),(ok||ok2)?`✓ Correct! ≈ ${(t*100).toFixed(1)}%`:`✗ Not quite — expected ≈ ${t<1?t.toFixed(4):t}`);}

/* ── BB84 Simulator ── */
const BASES=['+','x'],BITS=[0,1];
const STATE={'+0':'|0⟩','+1':'|1⟩','x0':'|+⟩','x1':'|−⟩'};

function runBB84(){
  const n=10;
  const alice={bits:[],bases:[],qubits:[]};
  const bob={bases:[],bits:[]};
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    alice.bits.push(BITS[Math.floor(Math.random()*2)]);
    alice.bases.push(BASES[Math.floor(Math.random()*2)]);
    alice.qubits.push(STATE[alice.bases[i]+alice.bits[i]]);
    bob.bases.push(BASES[Math.floor(Math.random()*2)]);
  }
  // Bob measures
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    if(alice.bases[i]===bob.bases[i]) bob.bits.push(alice.bits[i]);
    else bob.bits.push(Math.floor(Math.random()*2));
  }
  const matches=[];
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(alice.bases[i]===bob.bases[i]) matches.push(i);
  const key=matches.map(i=>alice.bits[i]);

  const cv=document.getElementById('cvBB84'); if(!cv)return;
  cv.width=cv.parentElement.clientWidth-2;
  const ctx=cv.getContext('2d'),W=cv.width,H=cv.height;
  ctx.clearRect(0,0,W,H);ctx.fillStyle='#fafaf8';ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
  const cols=['#2c5282','#c0392b','#1a6b3a','#b7530a'];
  const rowLabels=['Alice bit','Alice basis','Tx qubit','Bob basis','Bob bit','Match?'];
  const rowData=[
    alice.bits.map(String),
    alice.bases,
    alice.qubits,
    bob.bases,
    bob.bits.map(String),
    alice.bases.map((b,i)=>alice.bases[i]===bob.bases[i]?'✓':'✗')
  ];
  const cellW=(W-90)/n, cellH=28, startX=90, startY=18;
  ctx.font="10px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";
  rowLabels.forEach((l,r)=>{
    ctx.fillStyle='#888877';ctx.textAlign='right';ctx.textBaseline='middle';
    ctx.fillText(l,startX-6,startY+r*cellH+cellH/2);
    rowData[r].forEach((v,i)=>{
      const matched=alice.bases[i]===bob.bases[i];
      let bg='#fafaf8',fc='#1a1a14';
      if(r===5){bg=matched?'#d1fae5':'#fee2e2';fc=matched?'#1a6b3a':'#c0392b';}
      else if(matched&&r>=3){bg='#ebf0f8';}
      ctx.fillStyle=bg;ctx.fillRect(startX+i*cellW+1,startY+r*cellH+1,cellW-2,cellH-2);
      ctx.strokeStyle='#e8e4d8';ctx.lineWidth=0.5;ctx.strokeRect(startX+i*cellW,startY+r*cellH,cellW,cellH);
      ctx.fillStyle=fc;ctx.textAlign='center';
      ctx.fillText(v,startX+i*cellW+cellW/2,startY+r*cellH+cellH/2);
    });
  });
  // Column headers
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){ctx.fillStyle='#aaa';ctx.textAlign='center';ctx.fillText(i+1,startX+i*cellW+cellW/2,10);}
  const el=document.getElementById('bb84Info');
  if(el)el.textContent=`Matched positions: ${matches.map(i=>i+1).join(', ')}  →  Raw key: [${key.join('')}]  (${key.length} bits)`;
  const kl=document.getElementById('bb84KeyLen');
  if(kl)kl.textContent=`Key: ${key.length} bits`;
}

/* ── Eve canvas ── */
function updateEveCanvas(){
  const Np=parseInt(document.getElementById('slNp').value);
  document.getElementById('vNp').textContent=Np;
  const cv=document.getElementById('cvEve');if(!cv)return;
  cv.width=cv.parentElement.clientWidth-2;
  const ctx=cv.getContext('2d'),W=cv.width,H=cv.height;
  ctx.clearRect(0,0,W,H);ctx.fillStyle='#fafaf8';ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
  const PL=50,PR=20,PT=20,PB=40;
  const pW=W-PL-PR,pH=H-PT-PB;
  // Grid
  ctx.strokeStyle='#ede9dd';ctx.lineWidth=1;ctx.setLineDash([2,3]);
  [0.25,0.5,0.75,1].forEach(f=>{const y=PT+pH*(1-f);ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(PL,y);ctx.lineTo(W-PR,y);ctx.stroke();ctx.fillStyle='#bbb';ctx.font="9px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";ctx.textAlign='right';ctx.textBaseline='middle';ctx.fillText(Math.round(f*100)+'%',PL-4,y);});
  ctx.setLineDash([]);
  // Curve P(detect) = 1 - (3/4)^Np
  ctx.strokeStyle='#c0392b';ctx.lineWidth=2;ctx.beginPath();
  for(let n=1;n<=40;n++){const p=1-Math.pow(0.75,n);const x=PL+(n-1)/(39)*pW,y=PT+pH*(1-p);n===1?ctx.moveTo(x,y):ctx.lineTo(x,y);}
  ctx.stroke();
  // Current point
  const curP=1-Math.pow(0.75,Np);
  const cx2=PL+(Np-1)/39*pW,cy2=PT+pH*(1-curP);
  ctx.fillStyle='#c0392b';ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cx2,cy2,6,0,2*Math.PI);ctx.fill();
  ctx.fillStyle='white';ctx.beginPath();ctx.arc(cx2,cy2,2.5,0,2*Math.PI);ctx.fill();
  // Axes labels
  ctx.fillStyle='#888';ctx.font="10px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";ctx.textAlign='center';ctx.textBaseline='top';ctx.fillText('Published bits Nₚ',PL+pW/2,H-10);
  ctx.fillStyle='#555';ctx.font="bold 10px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";ctx.textAlign='left';ctx.fillText('P(detect Eve)',PL+5,PT+3);
  const el=document.getElementById('eveInfo');
  if(el)el.textContent=`Nₚ = ${Np} → P(detect) = 1 − (3/4)^${Np} = ${(curP*100).toFixed(3)}%`;
}

/* ── Key rate canvas ── */
function updateKeyRate(){
  const alpha=parseInt(document.getElementById('slAlpha').value)/100;
  document.getElementById('vAlpha').textContent=alpha.toFixed(2);
  const cv=document.getElementById('cvKeyRate');if(!cv)return;
  cv.width=cv.parentElement.clientWidth-2;
  const ctx=cv.getContext('2d'),W=cv.width,H=cv.height;
  ctx.clearRect(0,0,W,H);ctx.fillStyle='#fafaf8';ctx.fillRect(0,0,W,H);
  const PL=55,PR=20,PT=20,PB=40,pW=W-PL-PR,pH=H-PT-PB;
  const maxN0=10000;
  ctx.strokeStyle='#ede9dd';ctx.lineWidth=1;ctx.setLineDash([2,3]);
  [2500,5000,7500,10000].forEach(v=>{const y=PT+pH*(1-v/((1-alpha)/2*maxN0+1));if(y<PT||y>PT+pH)return;ctx.beginPath();ctx.moveTo(PL,y);ctx.lineTo(W-PR,y);ctx.stroke();ctx.fillStyle='#bbb';ctx.font="9px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";ctx.textAlign='right';ctx.textBaseline='middle';ctx.fillText(v,PL-4,y);});
  ctx.setLineDash([]);
  const maxKey=(1-0)/2*maxN0;
  ctx.strokeStyle='#2c5282';ctx.lineWidth=2;ctx.beginPath();
  for(let n=0;n<=maxN0;n+=100){const key=(1-alpha)/2*n;const x=PL+n/maxN0*pW,y=PT+pH*(1-key/maxKey);n===0?ctx.moveTo(x,y):ctx.lineTo(x,y);}
  ctx.stroke();
  ctx.fillStyle='#888';ctx.font="10px 'IBM Plex Mono',monospace";ctx.textAlign='center';ctx.fillText('N₀ (bits Alice generates)',PL+pW/2,H-6);
  ctx.fillText(`Key size = (1−${alpha.toFixed(2)})/2 · N₀ = ${((1-alpha)/2).toFixed(2)} · N₀`,PL+pW*0.5,PT+3);
}

window.addEventListener('load',()=>{
  setTimeout(()=>{runBB84();updateEveCanvas();updateKeyRate();},200);
});
</script>
